the right key with a canadian  typewriter collector now to the sudden  resignation of japan's longest-serving  prime minister  in an address to the nation shinzo abe  says he's stepping down for health  reasons  abe suffers from an inflammatory bowel  disease called  ulcerative colitis he says the illness  has gotten worse recently  and that he doesn't want his condition  getting in the way of decision-making  the 65-year-old was first elected as  prime minister in 2012  and today apologized for failing to  complete his term  in office atm style drug dispensers are  being installed in five locations across  canada to help address the opioid crisis  the machines provide safe doctor  prescribed medication to users  by scanning their palm the technology is  an extension of a pilot project that  began  in the center of the epidemic  vancouver's downtown east side  jessie thomas reports it was during a  panel talk in 2017 when the idea came to  dr mark tyndall he was speaking about  ways to tackle the overdose crisis  when it occurred to him why not provide  a safe supply of drugs  and vending machines the media picked up  on it fast  after maybe 20 interviews of  backtracking i  came to the conclusion that that was the  best idea i ever had  nearly 6 000 kilometers away in  dartmouth nova scotia cory yantha  had already been working on a prototype  dispensing machine designed to  distribute cannabis  he reached out to tyndall to see about  collaborating you know i'm not sure if  you thought about the technology  but we have a solution here that can  distribute these products to  registered users in a secure low barrier  way  last december the first my safe verified  identity dispenser was set up in  vancouver's downtown east side as part  of a safe drug supply pilot project  it looks like an atm but it's filled  with a prescription opioid called  hydromorphone  it scans users hands to access their  pills  we've dispensed over four thousand  packets of schedule and narcotics  among the 20 people enrolled in the  initial project tyndall says there were  no overdoses it's a promising result at  a time when leaders are searching for  solutions  between january 2016 and december 2019  more than 15 000 people died of apparent  opioid overdoses  and the crisis is escalating this past  may british columbia reported 174  suspected opioid overdose deaths  a record high that record was broken a  month later with 177 deaths  in july another 175 people died  tyndall says current opioid drug  strategies are failing and more must be  done  to provide users with a safe supply if  we're watching people  buying poison and just standing around  with naloxone kits trying to revive them  i mean  how many years do you want to do that  earlier this summer the company behind  the drug dispensing machines received a  five hundred thousand dollar loan from  atlantic canada opportunities agency to  expand  three more dispensers are being set up  in bc machines are also headed to london  ontario  and the location right here in dartmouth  nova scotia this project is needed in  a number of communities across canada  and we're looking at expanding this  further  the hope is that by making safe drugs  available these machines can help get  the crisis under control  jesse thomas global news dartmouth nova  scotia  if you're old school and still have a  typewriter you'll like this next story  one of canada's last remaining  typewriter technicians received a  special  thanks from tom hanks the hollywood  actor replied to a letter from  saskatoon's tom  tolowski who first wrote to hanks to  thank the movie star for being the  celebrity face  for typewriter collectors which he  believes has helped spark  interest and bring back business on  facebook chilowski said he's honored  and speechless up next the secret  ingredient in alberta's  new beer  scientists in india have detected  ultraviolet light from what is believed  to be one of the earliest galaxies in  the universe  the light was seen using astrosat  india's first  multi-wavelength satellite the findings  published by the journal nature  astronomy  could help explain the origin of light  after the big bang  brewers in calgary have discovered a  greener way to make beer but  one ingredient will get your attention  besides the hops and barley  mix in calgary's waste water the treated  brew meets the bar  set by alberta's health authorities but  as heather urex west explains it may  take some convincing to get people  sipping these suds okay  it's not the most appetizing of thoughts  to think that water that began here in  your shower or  toilet could end up in something you  might drink  but a calgary brewery insists their new  waste water beer is pretty good  a natural golden ale 4.5 alcohol and  it's a golden bubbly  craft beer the small batch craft brew  was born from a partnership with the  university of calgary  and xylem water solutions the goal here  is to show  that with proper treatment recycled  water can be put  to all kinds of new uses landlocked  countries globally  are already having to look at at water  reuse  you know just to irrigate and and  have water in their homes as you know  climate change progresses and there's  less glaciers melting into the rivers  waters is going to become a bigger  bigger deal  a third of the world's population is  currently living in areas where fresh  water resources are under stress a  number that is expected to double within  five years in singapore recycled water  is already being used routinely  treated wastewater currently meets more  than 30 percent of the country's water  needs  this was exciting for us just because of  the sustainability piece i mean the  beverage industry obviously uses a lot  of water so  any way we can kind of reuse and give  back is obviously exciting to us  of course tainted water can be very  dangerous any kind of contamination has  the potential to make people very sick  that's why the water used for these  brews was treated carefully  using processes guided by public health  the water  as i said went through some different  treatment technologies nano filtration  advanced oxidation then through  reverse osmosis the result  a waste water beer that has been so well  received  a week after its launch it's almost sold  out  heather urex west global news calgary  a drink to think about and that is  global national for this friday i'm  robin gill  tonight your canada is fork lake in  northern alberta  we would love to see your corner of the  country so please keep sending your  photos to viewers at globalnational.com  thank you for watching jeff semple will  be here tomorrow and i'll see you back  here on monday  have a great weekend  [Music]  you
 

